x86_64-windows  a true diamond - `Dia : D1, D2` where each of D1 and D2 names V virtually - is refused for this target, and the message is `'Dia' inherits a virtual base along more than one path, and x86_64-windows gives such a class one vbtable per path, which this compiler does not emit yet`. **The single-vbptr shapes landed on 2026-09-10** and are held by virtual-base-vbtable.cpp; this one did not, for a measured reason. cl gives such a class two tables and names them for the base each serves - `??_8Dia@@7BD1@@@ DD 00H, DD 028H` and `??_8Dia@@7BD2@@@ DD 00H, DD 018H`, where a class with one table gets the plain `??_8X@@7B@` - and the constructor stores each into its own subobject. Emitting one table under the plain name would leave a `D2 *` reading D2's own table, which says where V is in a D2 and not where it is here: a silent miscompile where this refuses by name. Measured on the Windows box, `vbfull.cpp`, 2026-09-10. What this case measures - that a virtual base is constructed once, by the most-derived class - is an Itanium question until the second table lands.
